//
// The reaper's job is orphaned scrape scratch tabs: tabs the human does not
// know exist. A tab a connector staged a draft into is the opposite — the
// human was pointed at it with a banner and a focus, and is expected to read
// and send it. Leaving it extension-owned meant the reaper force-closed the
// draft after EXTENSION_TAB_TTL_MS (measured 2026-08-02: staged 23:45:09, gone
// by 23:52:28) while the staging action still reported success, so nothing
// anywhere recorded the loss.
//
// Releasing rather than granting a longer lease is deliberate: any TTL is a
// countdown on work a human has not read yet, so a "long enough" number is
// just a rarer version of the same bug. After release the tab is an ordinary
// user tab — the reaper ignores it, close_tab refuses it (we should not be
// able to close a draft out from under someone), and the next staging run
// opens its own tab instead of reusing this one.
//
// Only call after the work is verified in the page. A run that dies before
// releasing leaves the tab owned, which is correct — that IS an orphan.
async function tool_release_tab(chromeApi, args, runCtx) {
	const tabId = args?.tab_id;
	if (typeof tabId !== "number") {
